 2. 🗳️ Religious left&#x27;s play for red-state conservatives 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 Illustration: Aïda Amer/Axios. Stock: Getty Images

 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 Democrats in a pair of deep-red states are testing the religious right's dominance by putting their Christian faith at the center of their campaigns — potentially creating a template for the party to compete in similar terrain, Axios' Avery Lotz and Russell Contreras write. 

 Why it matters: Upsets by Texas' James Talarico and Kansas' Adam Hamilton would all but ensure that Democrats take the Senate. 📊 A Pew poll released last week shows both an opening and an obstacle: 

 White evangelicals still strongly favor Republicans, while many other Christian groups lean Democratic or are more narrowly split. The big picture: Talarico, a Presbyterian seminary student, and Hamilton, a United Methodist pastor, are challenging conservatives' claim to speak for faith. Their bids come after Sen. Raphael Warnock (D-Ga.), senior pastor of the historic Ebenezer Baptist Church in Atlanta, was elected in 2021.

 President Trump won both Texas and Kansas by double digits in 2024. Neither state has sent a Democrat to the Senate in the 21st century — the last time Kansas did was in 1932. Both races this year are considered toss-ups . ⛪ Talarico's "religiosity is a way to soften his liberalism" and potentially win over Christians turned off by his scandal-plagued opponent , says Brandon Rottinghaus, who co-hosts Houston Public Media's "Party Politics."

 Hamilton has presented himself as a bridge-builder, drawing on his experience leading the largest United Methodist church in the nation, says Alexandra Middlewood, the political science department chair at Wichita State University. His well-known religious background could appeal to less engaged, moderate Christians and center-right voters, she says. The other side: Ralph Reed, chair of the conservative Faith and Freedom Coalition, tells Axios he's doubtful the Democrats' effort to lean into religion will attract voters of faith.

 "Voters will not vote for a candidate who professes to hold to their faith, but doesn't share their values," he says. Read on . 

 
 
