 3. 🚛 Can&#x27;t quit diesel 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 Illustration: Sarah Grillo/Axios. Stock: Getty Images

 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 The quickest and cheapest response to historically high diesel prices is also the simplest: Use less of it.

 Why it matters: That's easier said than done. Diesel is so entrenched in farming, trucking and other industries that even soaring prices aren't enough — at least not yet — to prompt a wholesale shift to alternatives like electric semi-trucks and manure-powered tractors, Axios' national energy correspondent Amy Harder reports. 🛢️ Catch up quick: Diesel averages $6.32 a gallon today, up 74% from a year ago, according to AAA . 

 Regular gasoline averages $4.29, up 39%. The big picture: Gasoline gets the attention, but diesel is the workhorse of the American economy. 

 It powers grocery trucks, tractors, freight trains, construction equipment, hospital backup generators and even school buses . 🍎 The price spike is hitting farmers during the fall harvest.

 "Farmers buy diesel by the truckload," says Curt Blades, senior vice president at the Association of Equipment Manufacturers. "So we're talking about hundreds of thousands of dollars in diesel, and when it goes up the way it has spiked, that's the difference between profit and loss in a year." President Trump announced $8 billion in one-time aid for farmers at a campaign rally in Kansas on Monday, where Republicans face a competitive Senate race.

