 5. 🛫 Higher fares, fewer flights 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 Illustration: Aïda Amer/Axios. Stock: Getty Images

 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 Soaring jet fuel prices are changing the airline industry — resulting in higher fares, more premium seats and fewer flight options, Axios' Nathan Bomey reports. 

 Airfares rose 22% year over year in September, marking a 10th straight monthly increase in the government's airfare price index , which hit an all-time high. 👀 All the major players are feeling the pain:

 American Airlines reported yesterday that its fuel bill jumped nearly 70% in the third quarter from a year ago, adding $1.9 billion in costs and taking significant steam out of its strong revenue growth. Southwest and United both slashed their profit forecasts this week on higher fuel costs. Delta expects its fuel bill this year to come in $6 billion above last year's. What we're watching: To protect their bottom lines, airlines are raising fares and using premium seats to squeeze more revenue out of their flights.

 At Southwest, the average passenger fare rose 26.9% to $231.69 in the third quarter, compared with a year earlier — and that doesn't include fees. Delta's premium seating revenue jumped 18% in the third quarter, even though the number of premium seats rose only 6%. Keep reading . 

 
 
