 8. 🥐 1 fun thing: Bakery next door 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 Illustration: Brendan Lynch/Axios

 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 Your neighborhood's next hot bakery — or coffee shop, pizza joint or BBQ spot — might be inside someone's house.

 Why it matters: More people are turning baking into a business, as online platforms and looser state regulations make it easier for home cooks to reach customers, Axios' Sami Sparber writes. 🧑‍🍳 By the numbers: Seller signups on home chef platform Hotplate doubled in July, co-founders Tenaya McCoy and Rishi Talati tell Axios.

 Over 1,000 sellers across 48 states joined Potluck , an "Airbnb for homemade food," within two months of launching this summer, says founder Ken Barton. Case in point: Fort Lauderdale baker Brooke Chambers turned her sourdough hobby into a micro-bakery after sharing loaves with family, friends and co-workers.

 She mixes dough on Wednesdays and bakes on Fridays for customer pickups, fitting 12-15 hours of bakery work around a full-time corporate job. Share this story . 

 
 
